---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title="Client Work by Michael Daum | Coobeeyon Development"
			description="Selected software and AI consulting work by Michael Daum, spanning construction planning, 3D design, semiconductor tools, HR software, and operations automation."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />

		<main class="longform">
			<h1>Client Work</h1>
			<p><i>Difficult products, built close to the people who use them.</i></p>

			<hr />

			<p>
				I work directly with clients from an uncertain problem through design,
				implementation, and the less glamorous work of making software reliable. These
				examples are anonymous until each client approves being named.
			</p>

			<h2>Interactive construction planning</h2>
			<p>
				<b>The problem:</b> Residential plans contain a great deal of geometry and intent,
				but the source drawings were not made to behave like structured product data.
				People need to compare options, understand a design, and catch problems without
				becoming experts in the underlying file formats.
			</p>
			<p>
				<b>My contribution:</b> I help build the path from complex drawing files to a rich
				browser workspace. That has included import and conversion, geometric data
				models, interactive plan views, option handling, publishing and recovery, and AI
				features that let people ask useful questions about a project.
			</p>
			<p>
				<b>The result:</b> What began as difficult source material can become a repeatable,
				inspectable planning workflow. Plans can be brought into the product, explored as
				structured choices, corrected, and published again without throwing away earlier
				work.
			</p>

			<h2>Three-dimensional electrical design</h2>
			<p>
				<b>The problem:</b> Generated electrical layouts are only useful when designers can
				understand them, investigate problems, and make precise changes inside a complex
				building model.
			</p>
			<p>
				<b>My contribution:</b> I work across the browser-based 3D application, from model
				loading and conversion through interaction and rendering. I have built and
				improved navigation, section cuts, transparent caps, outlines, cross-hatching,
				precise drawing and movement, constraint planes, snapping, orientation cues, and
				solution diagnostics.
			</p>
			<p>
				<b>The result:</b> Generated conduit routes are not trapped in a static viewer.
				Designers can inspect them in context, edit them with construction-aware tools,
				and see clearer feedback about constraints and possible quality problems.
			</p>

			<h2>Semiconductor test software</h2>
			<p>
				<b>The problem:</b> A mature C++ and Python product for semiconductor engineers had
				to keep evolving without discarding years of code, build infrastructure, and
				domain knowledge.
			</p>
			<p>
				<b>My contribution:</b> This was an advisory engagement, not implementation
				ownership. I reviewed architecture and live code with the engineering team,
				debugged parallel execution, built small examples to test design options, and
				advised on build and source-control tooling within the product's real constraints.
			</p>
			<p>
				<b>The result:</b> The team kept ownership of production changes while gaining
				concrete designs, focused prototypes, and an outside technical perspective they
				could apply to scalability and maintenance work.
			</p>

			<h2>AI-assisted human resources</h2>
			<p>
				<b>The problem:</b> An AI assistant for human-resources work has to reach useful
				information and take useful actions without confusing companies, people, or
				provider records. A persuasive answer is not enough; the surrounding system must
				be trustworthy.
			</p>
			<p>
				<b>My contribution:</b> I helped build document and employee workflows, realistic
				provider integrations, evaluation tools, and the tenant and identity safeguards
				that keep one organization's data and actions separate from another's.
			</p>
			<p>
				<b>The result:</b> The accepted work gave the product a broader, testable HR data
				surface and reliable ways to exercise AI workflows against realistic provider
				behaviour, while keeping authorization and organizational boundaries explicit.
			</p>

			<h2>Field-service operations automation</h2>
			<p>
				<b>The problem:</b> A field-service company may depend on separate systems for
				customers, technicians, purchasing, accounting, messages, and documents. The same
				real-world entity can appear differently in each one, and a careless automation
				can create duplicates or write to the wrong record.
			</p>
			<p>
				<b>My contribution:</b> I help build the platform and its guarded integrations. The
				work has included contextual company and people views, automatic imports with a
				human duplicate-review path, purchasing workflows, accounting synchronization,
				warranty research, and recovery for interrupted or ambiguous outside-system
				writes.
			</p>
			<p>
				<b>The result:</b> Shipped workflows now bring operational records into one useful
				workspace and preserve where each fact came from. Repeats, retries, and possible
				duplicates are treated as normal product conditions instead of reasons to risk a
				silent bad write.
			</p>

			<h2>Additional Work</h2>
			<h3>Custom mapping for a Squarespace site</h3>
			<p>
				As an unpaid favour for a friend, I built a custom Google Maps experience that
				helped visitors find participating restaurants and drop-off points, extending a
				Squarespace site beyond its standard tools.
			</p>

			<hr />

			<p>
				If you are working on a product with this kind of technical depth, <a
					href="mailto:mike.daum@coobeeyon.net">send me a note</a
				>.
			</p>
		</main>

		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
